eparate node rather than the same one. I'm weighing whether to intervene only on this node while the other stays at its prior, and whether self-consistency means setting the output to what A-FDT itself would return there, since the situation is symmetric.\n\nI'm working out whether the other copy's output would match ours by symmetry, but holding its node at prior under an intervention on ours still makes defection dominant. So the action-level A-FDT defects, and the self-consistent equilibrium is mutual defection, while the policy-level variant would cooperate. I'm less sure whether differing inputs that differ only by names should count as the same function, but the essay's Eq 4 treats the nodes as distinct, so I'll go with defect.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm looking at the twin prisoner's dilemma where each copy has different observations, and asking whether the two copies are still subjunctively dependent on each other. The other copy runs the same decision procedure on its own input, so it's a separate node rather than the same one. I'm weighing whether to intervene only on this node while the other stays at its prior, and whether self-consistency means setting the output to what A-FDT itself would return there, since the situation is symmetric.\n\nI'm working out whether the other copy's output would match ours by symmetry, but holding its node at prior under an intervention on ours still makes defection dominant. So the action-level A-FDT defects, and the self-consistent equilibrium is mutual defection, while the policy-level variant would cooperate. I'm less sure whether differing inputs that differ only by names should count as the same function, but the essay's Eq 4 treats the nodes as distinct, so I'll go with defect.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8931,"completion_tokens":110